# The generic attribute of a static route, `attribute "0x<code> 0x<flags> 0x<value>"`,
# is read through the daemon's own config load by textparse.ParseRawAttribute,
# the reader the update text uses. Each refused statement must fail validation
# and name what is wrong: flags RFC 4271 Section 4.3 forbids a sender, a
# statement that is not three hexadecimal words, a code Ze builds from another
# statement (NEXT_HOP, MP_REACH_NLRI, MP_UNREACH_NLRI, AS4_PATH, AS4_AGGREGATOR),
# and a MED
# whose value its wire decoder refuses. The last config carries a well-formed
# generic MED and must validate, so the refusals cannot widen into "attribute
# rejected". Needles carry no ':' because the directive splits on it. A value
# above 65535 octets is refused by the same reader; its 131072 hex digits do not
# fit one line of this file, so TestRouteAttributeGeneric proves that refusal
# through ParseRouteAttributes.
stdin=flags:terminator=EOF_FLAGS
bgp {
	router-id 203.0.113.253
	peer test-peer {
		connection {
			remote {
				ip 192.0.2.1
			}
			local {
				ip auto
			}
		}
		session {
			asn {
				local 65000
				remote 65001
			}
		}
		update {
			attribute {
				origin igp
				next-hop 192.0.2.254
				attribute "0x99 0x60 0x00000064"
			}
			nlri {
				ipv4/unicast add 10.0.1.0/24
			}
		}
	}
}
EOF_FLAGS
